      "problem": "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=9{a}_{n}-8{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=59,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using strong induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ is true for all {{field-3}} for {{field-4}}. In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}={{field-5}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1}={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={8}^{n}-5$ for all $n\\in N.$",
